    "case_summary": "A 56-year-old woman with a history of hypothyroidism and hyperlipidemia presented for follow-up. Medical history includes 20-year history of oral and ocular sicca symptoms, asymptomatic elevation of creatinine kinase levels with negative myositis and muscular dystrophy panel, negative muscle biopsy results, and chronic stable autoimmune thrombocytopenia. Previous medications included methotrexate and low-dose steroids. Currently on hydroxychloroquine 200 mg daily. Does not take calcium or vitamin D supplements. Review of systems was positive for mild dryness of mouth and throat and Raynaud phenomenon. No skin tightening, esophageal reflux, dysphagia, or telangiectasis reported.\n\nVital signs were within normal limits. Oral mucosa was dry. Painless pinpoint white nodules present in the subcutaneous plane of the right fifth finger. No lymphadenopathy, telangiectasis, skin lesions, or mucosal or skin ulcers observed. Joints had full range of motion without erythema, swelling, or tenderness. Remainder of physical examination findings were normal.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:\nHemoglobin: 13.0 g/dL (12-16)\nPlatelets: 115 × 1000/µL (150-440)\nLeukocytes: 4.9 × 1000/µL (3.6-11.0)\nNeutrophils: 47.4% (40.0-70.0)\nLymphocytes: 35.7% (20.0-50.0)\nSodium: 141 mmol/L (137-144)\nPotassium: 4.2 mmol/L (3.6-5.1)\nBUN: 15 mg/dL (8-24)\nCreatinine: 0.90 mg/dL (0.60-1.20)\nAutoimmune serology: Positive for antinuclear antibody 1:160 titer, nucleolar pattern, and anti-Ro\n\n- Imaging Studies:\nImage Title: Right Hand Radiograph\nImage Description: Soft-tissue calcifications in the distal first and fifth fingers\n\n- Pathology:\nPrevious skin biopsies showed no evidence of scleroderma\nBiopsy of digit lesions from right thumb and fourth finger consistent with calcinosis",
